# Day 33

**40km / 12 hours**

A long day for sure, not just in terms of kilometres but time taken. It started early since it would be worth getting the distance covered in the morning rather than the evening. I got pushed up and left around 6:15am, probably the earliest start so far. It began with a decent stretch on the road for an hour or two before getting into the proper walkway on the ridge. 

![A look at Hamilton and surrounding areas from a nice lookout](./lookout.avif)

The track began fairly gently as it was more of an easy day walk, climbing steadily with easy stairs and some nice lookouts and views of Kauri trees. However after this it began to get more difficult when the track quality was reduced. The ground was a little muddy but I was extremely fortunate in that it hasn't rained properly in a while so the mud was pretty dry. The other issue was the track being overgrown so having to wade through plants and step over tree roots to get through. Also there was still quite a lot of ascending and descending which was tough on the legs. This meant progress was agonisingly slow at this point.

![The beautiful trees making up the forest I was walking through](./forest.avif)

Eventually the track became a little nicer to walk and progress was more forthcoming even if there was still uphill sections along the way until I came to the highest point where there was a helipad and lookout. From then on there was a deluge of walkers coming in the opposite direction as we started down an inordinate number of stairs. It seemed to be endless, going on forever as there was over 1,000 stairs to descend about 300 metres. There were many day walkers climbing the steps for fitness reasons which is nice but it did make getting down a little tricky. 

![The walkway along the river](./river-walkway.avif)

The walkway being completed, there was a brief pit stop in the town to pick up some snacks before starting the river track which follows the Waikato river all the way to Hamilton and beyond. I don't need to go beyond though, at least for now so it was just to Hamilton which was easy enough walking but still a long way to travel so wore away at the time and ate up all afternoon. 

After hours and hours of walking the shared cycling/walking track, the houses alongside became more suburban and eventually urban as the city of Hamilton approached although each kilometre was a bit of a struggle by this point. Finally the walk came to an end as it generally does though.